<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	The app runs sandboxed, in its own data container, with no privilege
	beyond reaching the daemon.

	This is the architecture stated plainly in a signature: the app renders
	and nothing else, and every privileged operation — spawning a shell,
	owning a PTY, running as another user — happens in ighosttyd, which is
	already root and already authenticates its peers. An app that cannot
	spawn does not need the entitlements that make spawning possible, and an
	app that keeps its container keeps its preferences and its session ledger
	somewhere only it can write.

	So the whole list is: the marker the daemon authenticates against, and
	permission to look up the daemon's mach service. Nothing else.

	Entitlements deliberately NOT here, and what would bring each one back:

	- `platform-application` — grants nothing the app needs and actively
	  tightens its sandbox (it is what withholds IOKit user clients, and it
	  is why the app previously needed an exception list for Metal). Add it
	  back only if the bootstrap refuses to launch a non-platform binary,
	  and expect `com.apple.security.iokit-user-client-class` to become
	  necessary again in the same breath.
	- `com.apple.security.iokit-user-client-class` — the standard container
	  sandbox already permits the GPU user clients Metal opens; every App
	  Store app renders that way. Needed only alongside
	  `platform-application`. The symptom if it is missing is specific: a
	  black terminal that never reports a viewport, ghostty logging
	  `error.MetalFailed`, and the kernel logging
	  `deny(1) iokit-open-user-client AGXDeviceUserClient`.
	- `com.apple.private.security.no-sandbox` and its container-removing
	  siblings `no-container` / `container-required=false` — the sandbox is
	  the point now. `storage.AppDataContainers` went with them: it exists to
	  give a `no-sandbox` bundle its container back, and a sandboxed app
	  already has one.
	- `com.apple.private.skip-library-validation` — add back only if the app
	  fails to launch because a framework it loads is refused.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>wiki.qaq.ighostty.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.exception.mach-lookup.global-name</key>
	<array>
		<string>wiki.qaq.ighostty.service</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
